'''Baliscans''', in [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] {{lang|bl|baliscanos}} ([[w:Grammatical gender|masculine]]) or {{lang|bl|baliscanas}} ([[w:Grammatical gender|feminine]]), and colloquially known as '''''[[Costeño (nickname)|costeños]]''''' ("coast dwellers") or '''Islaños''' ("islanders") are people identified with the country of [[Balisca]]. This connection may be residential, legal, historical or cultural. For most Baliscans, several (or all) of these connections exist and are collectively the source of their being ''Baliscan''. Although citizens make up the vast majority of Balisca's population, non-citizen residents, dual citizens, and expatriates may also claim an Baliscan identity.

Balisca is a [[W:Polyethnicity|polyethnic]] and {{W|multilingual}} society, home to people of diverse [[w:Ethnicity|ethnic]], [[w:Religion|religious]], and [[w:Nationality|national]] origins, with the majority of the population made up of [[Kafto-Illypnia|Kafto-Illypnian]] migrants and their descendants. As a result, Baliscans do not equate their nationality with ethnicity, but with [[W:Citizenship|citizenship]]. Most often, however, the idea of ethnicity as it is understood in the Illypnian sense is not popular in the country. 

Aside from the [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous population]], nearly all Baliscans or their ancestors immigrated within the past five centuries. Among countries in the world that have received the most immigrants in modern history, Balisca, having recorded 23.6 million immigrants between 1870 and 1970, ranks first in the world, ahead of other settler colonies and immigrant destinations such as [[Markland]], [[Volistraya]] and [[Asikyira]].

